            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/postend-update-version",
                    "line": 43,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "This does not seem like something we should be modifying from outside the PLDM daemon.  Is there a way to handle this within the PLDM daemon itself?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/postend-update-version",
                    "line": 43,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "HenryWuQuanta",
                        "email": "Henry_Wu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "HenryWuQuanta"
                    },
                    "message": "Currently, the PLDM daemon does not provide a mechanism to defer or gate this behavior based on the CPU power state.\n\nIn Rainbow platform, some components are not ready until the host CPU is powered on. However, the PLDM daemon assumes the system is fully ready when the request is handled, which causes issues on this platform.\n\nDue to this limitation, we handle this logic outside the PLDM daemon for now."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/postend-update-version",
                    "line": 43,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "@manojkiran.eda@gmail.com, @paligill@gmail.com - Do you have any opinions?  I would expect the Software.Version to be read-only inside the PLDM daemon."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/postend-update-version",
                    "line": 43,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": ">> which causes issues on this platform.\n\nCan you enumerate what issues is observed?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/postend-update-version",
                    "line": 43,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "HenryWuQuanta",
                        "email": "Henry_Wu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "HenryWuQuanta"
                    },
                    "message": "Currently, the PLDM daemon does not provide a mechanism to refresh a device\u2019s version after a power event.\n\nIf we want to obtain the correct version through the PLDM daemon, there are two possible approaches:\n1. Connect to the PLDM devices only after the power is ready.\n\n2. Restart the PLDM daemon after the power is ready.\n\nHowever, both approaches have drawbacks.\n\nFor the first option, if a PLDM device reports an error event, the BMC may lose the connection to that device.\nFor the second option, restarting the PLDM daemon could affect other PLDM devices. If one of them is in the middle of a firmware update, it could potentially corrupt the device."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-santabarbara/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/postend-update-version",
                    "line": 43,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "HenryWuQuanta",
                        "email": "Henry_Wu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "HenryWuQuanta"
                    },
                    "message": "I have pushed new commits to resolve this issue.\n\nThis implementation uses the PLDM daemon to monitor D-Bus status changes.\nI also introduced a new property to indicate that this MMC device is not ready until the BIOS POST stage.\n\nWhen a BIOS POST event occurs, the PLDM daemon detects this change and checks whether the PLDM devices are MCTPI2CTarget or MCTPI3CTarget, then re-initializes them accordingly.\nThis allows the component version on the PLDM devices to be updated correctly.\n\nHere is the commit link:\n1. https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/entity-manager/+/88396\n2. https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/pldm/+/88403\n3. https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/entity-manager/+/88430\n\nDoes this better align with your expectations?"
                }
            ],
